re christmas  morrisons was the subject of a very  heated takeover battle between private  equity buyers it's been bought by  clayton de bilia and rice which is a  private equity firm interestingly  advised by sir terry leahy the former  tesco chief executive and now currently  run by david potts who is a former tesco  executive as well uh those businesses  have changed hands you could expect  there to be a fair bit of uh turmoil  within those businesses and a lot of  people incidentally analysts are  speculating that you could see prices  going up particularly at asda because  those businesses once they're owned by  private equity buyers they've both been  heavily uh  hocked up with debt in order for those  deals to go through well the buyers of  those businesses potentially will have  to claw back some of that repay that  debt down quickly potentially by putting  up prices and of course they have cover  to do this right now because there is  inflation in the market more generally  now potentially you'd think that would  leave tesco and sainsbury's as the two  stock market listed players a lot more  stability of ownership right now with a  lot more to play for in terms of cutting  prices and of course as i mentioned  you've got the german hard discounters  aldi and little sitting there in the  background right now it's a time of  great turmoil and shake-up in the  grocery industry and i think this is  part and parcel of that tesco really  wanting to sharpen up its act and  potentially turn the screw i think on  both asda and morrisons in particular
 

